<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Schweinfurth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Schweinfurth was a Baltic German botanist and explorer born in Riga. On his journey through the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> basin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> from 1868 to 1871 he reached the Uele river and became the first European to describe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a> and the Mangbetu, including the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munza">Munza</a>. His account, The Heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, was widely read.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-schweinfurth.jpg" alt="Georg Schweinfurth" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgschweinfurth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
